---
const aboutImg =
  "/images/c0c7fbaa-193d-489b-9ee9-f1e1aad1ac36.webp";
import ScrollReveal from "./ui/ScrollReveal.tsx";
---

<!-- 2-col about section -->
<section class="py-20 overflow-hidden">
  <ScrollReveal client:visible direction="up" className="max-w-7xl mx-auto grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 items-stretch">
    <!-- Left: text -->
    <div
      class="bg-[rgba(217,217,217,0.1)] flex flex-col justify-center px-6 sm:px-8 md:px-10 py-10 sm:py-12 order-2 md:order-1"
    >
      <h2
        class="font-bold text-xl sm:text-2xl uppercase leading-snug text-black mb-3 sm:mb-4"
      >
        About Us HEARST Book Publishing
      </h2>
      <p class="text-[#555] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Lorem Ipsum is simply dummy text of the printing and typesetting
        industry. Lorem Ipsum has been the industry's standard dummy text ever
        since 1966 when designers at Letraset and James Mosley, the librarian at
        St Bride Printing Library in London, took a 1914 Cicero translation and
        scrambled it to make dummy text for Letraset's Body Type sheets took a
        1914 Cicero translation. Lorem Ipsum is simply dummy text of the
        printing and typesetting industry. Lorem Ipsum has been the industry's
        standard dummy text ever since 1966 when designers at Letraset and James
        Mosley, the librarian at St Bride Printing Library.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Right: image -->
    <div class="order-1 md:order-2 h-64 sm:h-80 md:h-auto min-h-0">
      <img
        src={aboutImg}
        alt="About Hearst Book Publishing"
        class="w-full h-full object-cover"
      />
    </div>
  </ScrollReveal>
</section>

<!-- Extra paragraph below 2-col -->
<ScrollReveal client:visible direction="up" className="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-6 sm:py-8">
  <p class="text-[#555] text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
    Lorem Ipsum is simply dummy text of the printing and typesetting industry.
    Lorem Ipsum has been the industry's standard dummy text ever since 1966,
    when designers at Letraset and James Mosley, the librarian at St Bride
    Printing Library in London, took a 1914 Cicero translation.
  </p>
</ScrollReveal>
